CWE-22 is about Path Traversal, which occurs when an application allows users to provide input that can be used to traverse the directory structure of the operating system or file system.

Here's an example of CWE-22 path traversal vulnerability:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  const filename = req.query.filename;
  const filePath = `${__dirname}/${filename}`;
  fs.readFile(filePath, (err, data) => {
    if (!err) {
      res.send(data.toString());
    } else {
      res.status(404).send('File not found');
    }
  });
});
```

In this example, the `fs` module is used to read a file based on user input. This code can be vulnerable to path traversal attacks because it allows users to provide input that can be used to traverse the directory structure of the operating system or file system.

Here's how to safely validate a user-supplied filename in Node.js:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  const filename = req.query.filename;
  const allowedExtensions = ['.txt', '.pdf'];
  const extension = filename.split('.').pop().toLowerCase();
  if (allowedExtensions.includes(extension)) {
    const filePath = `${__dirname}/${filename}`;
    fs.readFile(filePath, (err, data) => {
      if (!err) {
        res.send(data.toString());
      } else {
        res.status(404).send('File not found');
      }
    });
  } else {
    res.status(400).send('Invalid file extension');
  }
});
```

In this example, we first get the filename's extension by splitting it at the last dot (.) and taking the last element of the resulting array. We then check if that extension is in our list of allowed extensions. If it is not an allowed extension, we return a 400 error with a message indicating that the file extension was invalid.

This code prevents path traversal attacks because it does not allow users to provide input that can be used to traverse the directory structure of the operating system or file system.

